From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan <ryan@esterlines.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 14:40:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] LibWeb+LibWebView+WebContent: Give an embedder's own pages a
 script bridge

A browser that serves pages of its own scheme, like extension pages, has
to give them an API that talks to the UI process, available from their
first line of script on. WebView::register_url_scheme() now takes an
optional URLSchemeBridge for that.

Before a document of such a scheme may run scripts, WebContent asks the
UI process for a bootstrap script and runs it as the body of a function
given the bridge: an object whose postMessage(string) reaches the UI
process, and whose onmessage receives what the UI process posts back.
The bridge is a parameter, so nothing new appears on the global object
unless the bootstrap puts it there.

The UI process answers only for documents it knows the asking page hosts
or is creating, and only if their origin is of a bridged scheme. It takes
a message's sender from its own records of the document, never from the
renderer, so a renderer can't speak for a document it doesn't host.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
